; defs.source.ud — INTERIM term-nailing sheet
; BANNER: This file is applicable ONLY until its content is integrated into
; def/ (one segment per term, full cadence) and it is retired by delete-test.
; Nothing here is canon by virtue of being here; it is the working surface
; where terms get nailed before earning their def/ segment.
;
; Conventions (same as the re-derivation sheets):
;   |t[TERM][SUB-TERM]  each |t nails one term (+ any sub-terms it defines)
;   **TERM**            bold at first use within its own definition
;   @[TERM]             reference to a nailed term
;   @{TERM}             flagged: load-bearing but not yet nailed
; Comment discipline: comments may cite, question, or record — they may NOT
; dress a phrase up as a term unless it is nailed or @{flagged}.
;
; Guideline in force: millennia of naming/mapping/scoping practice are the
; demand map for vocabulary — a coinage where an ancient word exists is a
; smell. External grounding used here: Néron, Tolmach, Visser & Wachsmuth,
; "A Theory of Name Resolution" (ESOP 2015) — ref/esop15/ — read whole; the
; borrowings are marked [SG] where the correspondence is exact enough to
; inherit their proofs.

;; ── The act ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

|t[REFERENCE-ACT] A **REFERENCE-ACT** is the production of narrowing material
  intended to determine a set of referents, made from an @[ORIGIN], carrying
  an @[EXPECTED-CARDINALITY]. The act is written at one moment and resolved
  at another — possibly elsewhere, by someone else, many times — and nothing
  guarantees the world holds still in between. That gap is constitutive, not
  incidental: every failure vocabulary below names a way the gap opens.
  :examples [a mailing address, a wikilink, an import statement, a URL, "the Joseph in accounting"]

|t[EXPECTED-CARDINALITY] Bounds {n,m} on how many referents a @[REFERENCE-ACT]
  intends. Part of the act, not of the resolver. A miss has meaning only
  against it: zero against {1,1} says the actor's model is wrong; three
  against {1,1} is @[AMBIGUITY]; zero against {0,N} is an answer.
  ; Independently arrived at by: SQL's scalar subquery (a {1,1} with only a
  ; hard error channel), hybrid logic's nominals ({1,1} in the semantics),
  ; wikilink stem lookup, and the steward's O15. That convergence is why
  ; this is nailed early despite being act-anatomy.
  ; NOT nailed here: the operational-vs-epistemic double bound. One bound,
  ; breadth first.

;; ── The two connection mechanisms ────────────────────────────────────

|t[BINDING][MINT][DANGLE][COLLIDE] A **BINDING** is a deliberate, maintained
  association from a name to a referent, held within one @[SCOPE] by whoever
  **MINTED** it or inherits its upkeep. The association — not any property
  of the referent — is the whole connection. Its failure modes are the
  maintenance's: a binding **DANGLES** (upkeep lost or never known here) or
  **COLLIDES** (more than one mint of the name in one scope).
  :examples [a filename, a phone-book entry, an import's declaration, a variable declaration, "Joseph" within a household]
  ; [SG] declaration. Collision is NOT defined away: duplicate bindings are
  ; representable, and what they produce is @[AMBIGUITY] at resolution —
  ; loud, first-class. Uniqueness-within-a-scope is a NORM a scope's
  ; maintainers may police (at mint time, the cheap place); it is not part
  ; of this definition. The refused-package incident is the postal system
  ; exhibiting exactly this: collisions exist; the virtue is surfacing them.

|t[MATCH] A **MATCH** is a connection established by testing candidates
  against supplied criteria at use time — the referents are whatever
  currently passes. Nothing was minted and nothing is maintained; the
  answer can differ every time the world's population differs.
  :examples [a glob, "the resident aged 47", a WHERE clause, a content hash (the test: bytes equal), a full-text search]
  ; The two mechanisms exhaust known addressing practice: every form we
  ; have examined decomposes into bindings consulted and matches run
  ; (positional index = match on current ordering; hash = match on content;
  ; forwarding pointer = binding held at the old endpoint). Falsifier: a
  ; mechanism that is neither. If found, this layer reorganizes.

|t[DESIGNATOR] Act-material that reaches its referent by @[BINDING].
|t[DESCRIPTION] Act-material that reaches its referents by @[MATCH].
  ; Derived vocabulary, kept because both words are ancient and useful.
  ; The classification is per-connection, not per-spelling: the same
  ; written form ("atom", a wikilink stem) is a DESIGNATOR where a scope's
  ; maintainers keep the binding and a DESCRIPTION over a population nobody
  ; polices. Kind follows maintenance.

;; ── Where bindings live ──────────────────────────────────────────────

|t[SCOPE] A **SCOPE** is the unit of resolution locality: it holds
  @[BINDING]s, it is where @[REFERENCE-ACT]s originate, and it connects to
  other scopes by labeled @[EDGE]s. The economics scopes exist for:
  bindings need only be unambiguous *locally*, and locally-cheap bindings
  compose into global reach.
  :examples [a household, a directory, a document's tree, a module, a namespace, the DNS root]
  ; [SG] scope — "a minimal region behaving uniformly with respect to name
  ; resolution", holding declarations and references, with labeled edges.
  ; A scope with no parent edge is a ROOT SCOPE; every scope reaches at
  ; least one root through containment. (Whether roots deserve the name
  ; WORLD, with a one-world-per-scope claim, is deliberately not nailed.)

|t[EDGE] A labeled connection from one @[SCOPE] to another, traversable
  during resolution. The universal label is **containment** (child scope →
  parent scope); other labels exist and are the extension point.
  :examples [lexical parent, module import, filesystem mount, "extends", a symlink's jump]
  ; [SG] the P (parent) and I (import) edges; their result that admissible
  ; label-sequences can be stated as a regular language over labels is
  ; inherited machinery, not restated here.

|t[LOCATION] A **LOCATION** is a @[SCOPE] that is itself the referent of a
  @[BINDING] — a named scope. Location-ness is a role a scope plays, not a
  new kind of thing.
  :examples [a directory (bound by its name in the parent), a city, a module, a keyed element in a document]
  ; [SG] "declaration with associated named scope" — their module concept,
  ; which they show is also a class, a package, a record: anything named
  ; that encapsulates bindings. Same generality claimed here.

;; ── Resolution ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

|t[ORIGIN] The @[SCOPE] a @[REFERENCE-ACT] resolves from. Constitutive, not
  optional: the same act-material yields different referents from different
  origins, legitimately.

|t[RESOLUTION] **RESOLUTION** derives, for a @[REFERENCE-ACT] at its
  @[ORIGIN], the set of referents its material determines — each result
  carried with its @[RESOLUTION-PATH]. Plurality is the normal case the
  machinery must represent: zero, one, and many are all outcomes, given
  meaning by the act's @[EXPECTED-CARDINALITY].

|t[RESOLUTION-PATH] The sequence of @[EDGE] traversals and @[BINDING]
  consultations that justifies one resolution result. The path is the
  evidence; folding a result down to its bare referent discards exactly the
  justification, and is a choice, not a default.
  ; [SG] resolution paths. Proof-inheritance pointer, corrected 2026-08-09:
  ; ESOP 2015's Theorem 1 is proven only for the FIXED policy (WF = P*·I*,
  ; fixed order) — the paper itself says the algorithm does not cover the
  ; §2.5 variants. Since EDGE declares labels the extension point, the
  ; proofs this theory actually needs are the generalized metatheory:
  ; van Antwerpen et al., "Scopes as Types" (OOPSLA 2018) and Rouvoet et
  ; al. (OOPSLA 2020) — neither yet in ref/, neither yet read here.
  ; Unverified until a def/ segment drafts against them.

|t[WALK-STEP][MEDIATED-STEP] Two kinds of step in a @[RESOLUTION-PATH]. A
  **WALK-STEP** traverses a standing @[EDGE] — no resolution needed to take
  it. A **MEDIATED-STEP** must first *resolve a name* to obtain its edge:
  the step exists only through a sub-resolution.
  :examples-mediated [an import, a symlink, a forwarding address, "ask the registry where it lives now"]
  ; [SG] P-steps vs I-steps, with the two disciplines that come with the
  ; distinction: (a) termination — a mediated step's sub-resolution may not
  ; use the step it enables (their seen-imports set); (b) admissibility —
  ; policies constrain WHERE mediated steps may appear in a path (their
  ; WF ∈ P*·I* : walk first, mediate after — no walking onward from inside
  ; a mediated jump's lexical context). Both are inherited, load-bearing,
  ; and the reason "some crossings take execution" is structure, not vibes.

|t[ADMISSIBILITY][PREFERENCE] The declared parts of a resolution policy
  nailed here are two. **ADMISSIBILITY**: which path shapes count at all —
  a constraint over @[EDGE]-label sequences. **PREFERENCE**: a declared
  order among admissible *results* — shadowing ("nearer beats farther",
  "local beats imported") made inspectable. Both belong to the act, not
  globally to the resolver.
  ; [SG] WF predicate + specificity ordering (2015); verified 2026-08-09
  ; against the modern formulation (Zwaan & Bach Poulsen, ECOOP 2024, §3 —
  ; ref/zwaan-2024-defining/): the split survived and was PROMOTED from one
  ; global policy pair to per-query parameters (label-regex + label order,
  ; alongside a data predicate that corresponds to the act's own narrowing
  ; material). Per-act placement above is therefore current state of the
  ; art, not a 2015 artifact. Preference is deliberately over RESULTS, not
  ; labels — their Appendix A.1 shows label orders insufficient (Java's
  ; inaccessible-inherited-field case forces a path-level order).
  ; Why nailed this early: it reconciles "never a silent best-guess" with
  ; real systems that DO prefer — a pick under a declared preference is
  ; disclosed by construction; a pick without one is a silent guess.
  ; These two are NOT claimed exhaustive of policy — see ACCESSIBILITY in
  ; the not-nailed list below.

|t[AMBIGUITY] More than one maximally-@[PREFERENCE]-preferred resolution
  result. An outcome, not an error: ambiguity is what insufficient
  narrowing IS, and it is surfaced with its candidate set.
  ; The postal on-ramp's one-liner survives verbatim; it earned it.

;; ── Deliberately NOT nailed (named so they cannot smuggle) ───────────
; ACCESSIBILITY — a third policy component, formalized in the same research
;   line (ECOOP 2024): a predicate over the ⟨origin, path, declaration⟩
;   triple ("may THIS asker, via THIS route, reach THIS thing") that
;   neither label-sequence admissibility nor result preference expresses —
;   and that can participate IN preference (an inaccessible nearer result
;   letting a farther one win). Not exotic in our domain: file permissions,
;   DNS split-horizon, "who in the household may be told." The right
;   factoring is open even in the literature; nailed later, on demand.
; WORLD / root-scope naming and the one-world-per-scope claim.
; ADDRESS and DIRECTIONS as path classifications (all-walk vs mediated-
;   bearing is the candidate cut; the old progressive-scoping texture may
;   or may not survive it).
; PATH itself as a first-class term distinct from RESOLUTION-PATH (the
;   written-artifact sense: material that DENOTES a route).
; ENTITY vs VALUE-OBJECT; the four promises; canonicity.
; Moments, versions, as-of; the entity-that-moves ladder (vacated vs
;   dangled).
; The operational/epistemic double bound; selection policies.
; REFERENT / TARGET-SET seating.
